@article{ WOS:000080786300004,
Author = {Hulme, PA and Blegen, MA},
Title = {Residential status and birth outcomes: Is the rural urban distinction
   adequate?},
Journal = {PUBLIC HEALTH NURSING},
Year = {1999},
Volume = {16},
Number = {3},
Pages = {176-181},
Month = {JUN},
Abstract = {In studies comparing the birth outcomes of rural and urban women,
   residency status is frequently defined dichotomously as either rural or
   urban. Since residency status appears to be a continuum, however, the
   usefulness of other categorization systems needs to be explored. The
   purpose of this study was to compare birth outcomes using a three-level
   variable for residency status (rural, rural adjacent to urban, and
   urban). The study population was comprised of women who delivered by
   cesarean section over an 18-month period (N = 263) at a tertiary care
   hospital. Data were collected from patient charts, interviews, and the
   hospital information system. Residency status was determined by county
   of residence. Birth outcomes examined included, gestational age,
   birthweight, Apgar scores, maternal complications, length of hospital
   Stay, and costs of hospital care. Rural women had worse birth outcomes
   overall and traveled the greatest distance for delivery. Rural-adjacent
   women had the best birth outcomes of the three groups, yet were the
   youngest, least educated, least likely to be married, and the least
   likely to be privately insured. By using a nondichotomous three-level
   variable for residency status, two distinct groups of rural women were
   identified whose maternal health care needs may differ from each other.
   Access to maternal health care in rural areas is a national concern. One
   method of determining whether rural women are receiving adequate
   maternal care is to compare their birth outcomes with those of urban
   women. Categorizing the residential status of these women, however, can
   be problematic. The arbitrary nature of defining where rural ends and
   urban begins is demonstrated by the nonidentical populations found. in
   ``rural areas,{''} as defined by the U.S. Bureau of the Census (U.S.
   Department of Commerce, Bureau of Census, 1991), and in ``statistical
   nonmetropolitan areas,{''} as defined by the Office of Management and
   Budget (Office of Technology Assessment, 1990).
   Because rural is more a continuum than a homogenous category,
   nondichotomous, categorization systems of residential status have been
   proposed. For example, the Rural-Urban Continuum Codes developed by the
   U.S. Depart ment of Agriculture divide counties into 10 categories based
   on population clustering and proximity to urban counties; there are six
   categories of rural and four categories of urban(Butler, 1990). Pickard
   proposed 11 categories of counties, six rural and five urban, that take
   into account total population, largest city or town, and commuting
   patterns (1988). A simpler categorization system was proposed by the
   Iowa Governor's Task Force on Rural Health (1989) with two categories of
   rural and one category of urban, based on population and commuting
   patterns.
   The usefulness of these categorization systems for studying rural
   women's birth outcomes has not been well established. Therefore, the
   purpose of this exploratory study was to use the categorization system
   proposed by the Iowa Governor's Task Force on Rural Health (1989) to
   compare the birth outcomes of 263 women who delivered by cesarean
   section over an 18-month period at a large hospital in Iowa. The three
   categories for residency status were rural, rural adjacent to urban, and
   urban. In addition, maternal characteristics that may affect outcomes
   were compared according to residential status.
   The data for this research came from a study on multidisciplinary
   managed care that focused on cesarean births (Blegen, Reiter, Goode, \&
   Murphy, 1995). The site for this study was the University of Iowa
   Hospital and Clinics (UIHC), which is an academic health center as well
   as a state-funded tertiary care center. Besides serving local women
   regardless of risk or insurance status, UIHC serves women with high-risk
   pregnancies and women from eastern Iowa who have no private health
   insurance. The following research questions were addressed:
   1. Do rural, rural adjacent to urban (rural-adjacent), and urban women
   differ by the following birth outcomes: gestational age, birthweight,
   Apgar scores, maternal complications, length of hospital stay, and costs
   of hospital care?
   2. Are there differences in maternal characteristics (age, marital
   status, education, comorbidity, prenatal care, distance traveled for
   delivery, and insurance status) among the rural, rural-adjacent, and
   urban women who participated in this study?
   3. What are the relationships among the birth outcomes and maternal
   characteristics used for this study?},
